\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The cup product and the unweighted adjoint fail}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Subdivide the ordered edge with vertices #{0<m<1} into the
edges #{(0,m)} and #{(m,1)}. Let #{\alpha} be the vertex cochain
with #{\alpha(0)=0} and #{\alpha(m)=1}, and let #{\beta} be the
edge cochain with value #{0} on #{(0,m)} and value #{1} on
#{(m,1)}. The cup product of \ref{fdip-0093} reads
##{(\alpha\cup\beta)(0,m)=\alpha(0)\beta(0,m)=0,}
##{(\alpha\cup\beta)(m,1)=\alpha(m)\beta(m,1)=1.}
The dual of subdivision sums these two values and gets #{1}.
The pulled vertex cochain has value #{\alpha(0)=0} at the
coarse initial vertex, and the pulled edge cochain has value
#{0+1=1}. Their cup product is #{0}. The two results differ.}

\p{On the cycle of length #{6}, let the coarse vertices be the
even residues and let #{a} equal #{1} on the fine edge from
#{0} to #{1} and equal #{0} on every other fine edge. The dual
of subdivision sends #{a} to the coarse cochain with value
#{1} on the edge from #{0} to #{2} and value #{0} on the other
two coarse edges. The counting adjoint of \ref{fdip-00OL} at
the coarse vertex #{2} is the incoming value minus the outgoing
value, hence #{1}. The same adjoint on the fine cycle vanishes
at vertex #{2}, because the only nonzero edge ends at vertex
#{1}. Pulling that fine adjoint back to the coarse vertex
#{2} gives #{0}.}
